{
  "result": "I've been thinking lately about the things that don't photograph well.\n\nNot sunsets. Not weddings. Not the big, announced moments with good lighting and everyone facing the same direction. I mean the other stuff \u2014 the stuff that actually holds a life together.\n\nSomeone peeling an orange for someone else because they hate getting the pith under their fingernails. A coat left on a chair to save a seat, long after it's obvious the person isn't coming back for a while. Remembering, without being reminded, that your friend takes her tea so strong it\u2019s almost rude, and making it that way anyway. Texting \"let me know when you get home\" and then actually staying awake until they do.\n\nNone of it looks like anything. If you tried to take a picture, you'd just have a blurry orange. A coat.\n\nI think about how much of love, real love \u2014 not the movie kind, the maintenance kind \u2014 is just paying very close attention for a very long time. Learning the sound of someone's key in the door when they're tired versus when they're not. Knowing which story your dad is about to tell by the way he clears his throat. Knowing not to ask \"are you okay?\" but to just sit down on the floor next to them, shoulder to shoulder, looking at the same wall.\n\nThere's a woman I imagine \u2014 I must have seen her once and then built a whole life for her in my head \u2014 who waters her neighbor's plants when he's in the hospital. Not just waters them, but turns them a quarter-turn toward the sun every few days, so they don't lean. He will never know she did that part. The plants won't tell him. She does it because she knows he would.\n\nThat feels important to me. The quarter-turn.\n\nThe world tells us to be remarkable. To be loud, to be first, to leave a mark. But most of us will leave a different kind of mark \u2014 not a statue, but a series of warmed seats, watered plants, remembered preferences. We will be the reason someone else's ordinary Tuesday was slightly less lonely.\n\nI like to think kindness accumulates like dust in sunlight. You can't point to one mote and say, \"There. That changed everything.\" But breathe it in long enough, live in a house where it's floating around, and it changes the air.\n\nSo if I could wish for anything completely impractical and unambitious, it wouldn't be to be unforgettable. It would be to be someone who learns how to do the quarter-turn well. To notice. To remember. To do the small, unphotographable thing, and then not need anyone to have seen it.\n\nExcept, maybe, the plant.",
